In article <jstone.0704@hotcity.UUCP> jstone@hotcity.UUCP (John Stone) writes:
>Seeing as how well my wish question got answered,  here's another great
>debatable question:
>        1)  What is the best monster to polymorph into when you have both a
>            ring of polymorph and a ring of polymorph control?
>

If you have armor on, a vampire lord is a good choice.  They are strong,
they fly over traps and water (but can still pick up items on the floor).
They are a particularly good choice when drawing the Wiz down in levels
because their bite is like Stormbringer's bite.

If you are careful to take your favorite cloak and armor off first, you
will find being a xorn very useful at times.  It allows you to walk through
walls.  Makes the endgame trivial too (A loophole that IMHO ought to be
closed).

There used to be trick of polymorphing into dragon, laying a few eggs,
letting the hatchlings duke it out (use  a ring of conflict) and eating
the corpse to gain intrinsics.  This won't work for baby dragons anymore,
but if you are patient, you can grow your babies into the full-size model
and use the trick.  Its a pain, and when I have bothered with it, it is
only with black dragons, to get disintegration resistance.

>Also, here's another question dealing with polymorph:
>        2)  I've seen people like wands of polymorph alot... why?  I
>            personally see no reason to have one other than to change your pet
>            or get rid of powerfull monster.  If it has to deal with
>            polypiling, (and my previous question on P.P hasn't been answered)
>            what excatly is polypiling?

At the risk of being one of many to answer: "polypiling" refers to zapping
a wand of polymorph at a large pile of objects, in order to potentially
pick up very useful items.  Since orcs and other messy creatures leave a
lot of junk (especially armor and weapons) around, the diligent explorer
can drag stuff from 10-12 levels into several adjacent piles and proceed
to acquire all sorts of goodies.  It's kinda fun...once.

Commentary (er..flame) Alert:

I just won a game where I found a wand of polymorph on level 5.  Before
I ever had to go below level 12, my originally wimpy wizard had:
(Patch Lvl 9)

  STR: 18/55 DEX: 22 CON: 18  INT:20 WIS: 19 CHR: 10 + most intrinsics
  blessed +2 speed boots
  +0 shield of reflection
  blessed amulet of life saving
  +4 gauntlets of dexterity (rustproofed)
  +2 helm of brilliance (rustproofed)
  +0 helm of opposite alignment  <--- very handy !!
  +8 war hammer named Mjollnir
  +0 broadsword named Stormbringer
  15 potions of holy water
   3 blessed magic markers
   blessed luckstone
   pickaxe, stethoscope, mirror, bag of holding, etc.

   plus I left a pile that had Fire Brand, Orcrist, +6 Excalibur, Grimtooth,
    Cleaver, Sting, and Sunsword in it.

All of this took 1 altar and 1 wand of polymorph, and the game was certainly
boring after that.  In fact the only thing that made the game interesting
was the exercise to show just HOW unbalancing polypiling really is.

The very first game I ever won, I never did find a wand of polymorph, and
didn't wish for one with the castle's wand of wishing.  I've won games with
and without since, but I have found the non-polypile wins far more
satisfying.   After this last demonstration, I've sworn off polypiling
for good - The sole exception wil be the creation of dragon scale mail
from dragon corpses.

Some people really like polypiling.  Fine.  Lets leave it in for them,
but limit it to three items per zap for #ifdef HARD games.   
